ead to us, but our hand isn’t great for maintaining lead long since we have many disjointed cards.  \n   - **Play a high single** (♣K, ♣A, ♥2, RedJoker): Overkill, wastes valuable cards on a weak trick.  \n   - **Pass**: Let teammate handle it. If teammate can’t beat it, landlord gets trick but only with a 4 — still cheap for us. Possibly smarter to conserve power for later bombs/rocket when landlord plays stronger.\n\nGiven landlord’s card count and the fact ♣4 is so low, the best team play is often to pass and let teammate try to take it with a small card if possible. This conserves our high cards for crucial moments.\n\n**Reason**:  \nPassing is optimal because:  \n- The landlord’s play is weak; even if teammate passes and landlord wins, they gain minimal advantage.  \n- We keep all options open: we still have the triplet of 3s (potential bomb) and RedJoker to beat bombs/rocket later.  \n- Our hand has many low singles that we may need to dump later under teammate’s lead, but if we take the lead now, we’ll have to play out those low singles under our own lead, which may allow landlord to regain control with stronger singles.  \n- In the climbing game, it’s often correct to pass early weak plays to save power, especially when you have a teammate after you.\n\n**Answer**:  \n{"cards": []}'}
